Recently, I attended a DevOps interview where I was asked an interesting question focused on pod security.
In Kubernetes, we often run multiple pods, but sometimes only one pod should have access to an AWS S3 bucket.
How would you achieve this,
Given Scenario
You have an Amazon EKS cluster running multiple pods:
1. frontend-pod – serves the UI
 2. auth-pod – handles authentication
 3.worker-pod – processes files and uploads them to Amazon S3
Requirement:  Only worker-pod should access the S3 bucket.  All other pods must have NO AWS permissions.

Solution: EKS Pod Identity Agent
Following steps need to perform:
1 .Create an IAM Role with required S3 permissions.
2.Enable Pod Identity Agent in the EKS cluster
3.Create a Pod Identity Association
4.Attach the IAM role only to worker-pod
5.Other pods remain completely isolated
After that interviewer asked cross question me Why Choose EKS Pod Identity Agent?
 1. Pod-level IAM access
 2. No hardcoded AWS credentials
 3. Least-privilege security model
 4. AWS-managed & simpler than IRSA
